":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,641,279,7071,6839,11,400,1706,28,16,18,3,323,400,5626,28,17,3,8153,13,220,3555,374,279,3084,315,279,10238,400,9548,3,30,17399,697,4226,304,44548,17855,1352,382,58,6405,921,7633,1188,15,11,20,29621,7,16,17,11,20,1106,7633,1188,16,17,11,20,29621,7,16,17,11,15,1106,7633,1188,16,17,11,15,29621,7,15,11,20,1106,7633,1188,16,17,11,20,29621,7,16,15,13,16,11,15,13,23,1106,7633,1188,16,15,13,19,23,11,16,13,21,19,29621,7,24,13,19,22,11,17,13,15,21,1106,7633,1188,24,13,19,22,11,17,13,15,21,29621,7,24,13,15,24,11,16,13,17,17,1106,7633,1188,16,16,11,20,29621,7,16,16,11,19,1106,7633,1188,16,16,11,19,29621,7,16,17,11,19,1106,1502,445,32,44970,15,11,20,701,54,317,1502,445,33,44970,16,17,11,20,701,36,317,1502,445,34,44970,16,17,11,15,701,36,317,1502,445,35,44970,16,15,13,16,11,15,13,23,701,16844,317,24157,6405,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIn the figure shown, $AC=13$ and $DC=2$ units.  What is the length of the segment $BD$? Express your answer in simplest radical form.\n\n[asy]\ndraw((0,5)--(12,5));\ndraw((12,5)--(12,0));\ndraw((12,0)--(0,5));\ndraw((12,5)--(10.1,0.8));\ndraw((10.48,1.64)--(9.47,2.06));\ndraw((9.47,2.06)--(9.09,1.22));\ndraw((11,5)--(11,4));\ndraw((11,4)--(12,4));\nlabel(\"A\",(0,5),W);\nlabel(\"B\",(12,5),E);\nlabel(\"C\",(12,0),E);\nlabel(\"D\",(10.1,0.8),SW);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2619107212cad062fc93c8bad53cf5a71dcf13a5fc7b40c06729687d1643156e"},"seconds":23.762135982513428,"seed":216713268630545913,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we need to use the Pythagorean theorem in two right triangles that are formed within the diagram. The first triangle is $\\triangle ABC$, and the second is $\\triangle BDC$.\n\nFirstly, consider $\\triangle ABC$ with $AC = 13$ units and $BC = 12$ units. We can calculate the length of $AB$ using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n\\[ AC^2 = AB^2 + BC^2 \\]\n\nPlugging in the known values:\n\n\\[ 13^2 = AB^2 + 12^2 \\]\n\n\\[ 169 = AB^2 + 144 \\]\n\n\\[ AB^2 = 169 - 144 \\]\n\n\\[ AB^2 = 25 \\]\n\n\\[ AB = \\sqrt{25} \\]\n\n\\[ AB = 5 \\]\n\nSo, the length of $AB$ is 5 units.\n\nNext, consider $\\triangle BDC$ with $BC = 12$ units, $DC = 2$ units, and we are looking for the length of $BD$. Using the Pythagorean theorem again:\n\n\\[ BC^2 = BD^2 + DC^2 \\]\n\nPlugging in the known values:\n\n\\[ 12^2 = BD^2 + 2^2 \\]\n\n\\[ 144 = BD^2 + 4 \\]\n\n\\[ BD^2 = 144 - 4 \\]\n\n\\[ BD^2 = 140 \\]\n\n\\[ BD = \\sqrt{140} \\]\n\n\\[ BD = \\sqrt{4 \\times 35} \\]\n\n\\[ BD = 2\\sqrt{35} \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of $BD$ is $\\boxed{2\\sqrt{35}}$."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2\\sqrt{35}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
